(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-05"] = {"1":{"s":"A microprocessor is a CPU on a single chip. The first was the 4-bit Intel 4004 in 1971, followed by the 8-bit 8008, 8080 and 8085, the 16-bit 8086 and the 32-bit processors. Each generation has a wider word, a faster clock and more memory. LSI and VLSI technology put thousands to millions of transistors on one chip. Upward software compatibility means a program for an older chip runs on a newer one of the family.","w":["The Intel 4004 was the first microprocessor, and it was 4-bit.","Upward compatibility means old programs run on the new chip, not the other way round."],"x":["Before the microprocessor, the CPU was made of many separate parts. In 1971 Intel put the whole CPU on one chip, the 4-bit Intel 4004. Then came 8-bit chips (8008, 8080, 8085), 16-bit chips (8086) and later 32-bit and 64-bit chips.","In each generation the word length (the bits handled together), the clock speed and the memory that can be addressed grew, and the cost per computing power fell. This was possible because of integration: LSI (large scale integration) has thousands of transistors on a chip and VLSI (very large scale integration) has hundreds of thousands to millions.","Upward software compatibility means that a program written for an older microprocessor of a family also runs on the newer one without change; the 8085 runs 8080 programs. It protects the money spent on software."],"e":"A 4-bit chip handles 4 bits at a time, an 8-bit chip like the 8085 handles 8 bits, so the 8085 can do in one step what the 4004 needed several steps for."},"2":{"s":"A microprocessor is the CPU of a microcomputer made on one chip by VLSI technology. It reads instructions from memory, decodes them and does arithmetic, logic and control work. With memory and input and output chips it forms a microcomputer. It works with three buses: a data bus, an address bus and a control bus.","w":["A microprocessor is only the CPU; a microcomputer also has memory and I/O.","The address bus carries addresses one way; the data bus carries data both ways."],"x":["A microprocessor is a single chip that does the work of the CPU of a computer. It fetches an instruction from memory, decodes it to find what is wanted, and executes it, and then fetches the next one.","A microprocessor alone cannot form a computer. With memory (ROM and RAM) and input and output devices it forms a microcomputer. The parts are joined by three buses: the data bus carries data (in both directions), the address bus carries the address of a memory location or device (in one direction), and the control bus carries signals such as read and write.","The speed of a microprocessor depends on its clock frequency and its word length. Examples: Intel 8085 (8 bit) and 8086 (16 bit)."],"e":"When you press a key, the microprocessor reads the key code from the input device through the data bus, after sending the address of the device on the address bus and a read signal on the control bus."},"3":{"s":"A generic microprocessor has an ALU (arithmetic and logic unit), an accumulator, general purpose registers, a flag register, the program counter, the stack pointer, the instruction register with the instruction decoder, and the timing and control unit, joined by an internal data bus. The program counter holds the address of the next instruction; the stack pointer holds the address of the top of the stack.","w":["The instruction register holds the opcode; the decoder works out what it means.","The timing and control unit makes the read, write and other control signals."],"x":["Inside the microprocessor the main blocks are: the ALU, which does arithmetic (add, subtract) and logic (AND, OR, XOR, compare); the accumulator, the main register used with the ALU; the flag register, whose bits tell about the result; the general purpose registers; the program counter and the stack pointer.","The program counter (PC) holds the address of the next instruction to fetch and increases after each fetch. The stack pointer (SP) holds the address of the top of the stack, the part of memory used to save return addresses and data. The instruction register holds the opcode of the instruction that was fetched, and the instruction decoder finds out which operation is wanted. The timing and control unit then makes the control signals to carry it out.","A data buffer sits between the outside data bus and the internal bus. The units are joined by the internal data bus. The time for fetching and executing one instruction is the instruction cycle, made of machine cycles."],"e":"To add two numbers: the PC gives the address, the opcode of ADD is fetched into the instruction register, the decoder finds that it is an add, the control unit tells the ALU to add, and the flags show the result."},"4":{"s":"The 8085 is an 8-bit, 40-pin microprocessor on a +5 V supply with a 16-bit address bus (64 KB of memory). Its parts: ALU, accumulator, temporary and flag registers, registers B, C, D, E, H, L and the temporary W and Z, program counter, stack pointer, instruction register and decoder, timing and control, interrupt control and serial I/O control. The flags are Sign, Zero, Auxiliary carry, Parity and Carry.","w":["The 8085 has 5 flags: S, Z, AC, P and CY.","The 16-bit address bus gives 2 to the power 16, that is 65,536 locations, so 64 KB of memory."],"x":["The 8085 is an 8-bit microprocessor made by Intel, with 40 pins, a single +5 V supply and a clock that is half of the crystal frequency (a 6 MHz crystal gives 3 MHz). The data bus is 8 bits and the address bus is 16 bits, so it can address 64 KB.","Registers: the accumulator A, the general purpose registers B, C, D, E, H and L (usable in pairs BC, DE and HL), temporary registers W and Z (not available to the programmer), the 16-bit program counter and stack pointer, and the flag register. The flags are: Sign (set if the result is negative), Zero (set if the result is zero), Auxiliary carry (a carry from bit 3 to bit 4), Parity (set if the number of 1s is even) and Carry (a carry out of bit 7).","Other blocks: the instruction register and decoder, the timing and control unit, the interrupt control (TRAP, RST 7.5, 6.5, 5.5 and INTR) and the serial I/O control (SID and SOD). The lower address byte shares the pins AD0 to AD7 with the data, which is the multiplexed bus."],"e":"Adding 10H and 28H in the accumulator gives 38H. The flags are all 0: the result is positive and not zero, there is no carry, and 38H has three 1s, which is odd parity, so P is 0."},"5":{"s":"The 8085 pins are in six groups: the address bus (A8 to A15), the multiplexed address and data bus (AD0 to AD7), control and status signals (ALE, RD, WR, IO/M, S0, S1), power and frequency signals (Vcc, Vss, X1, X2, CLK OUT), interrupt and externally initiated signals (TRAP, RST 7.5, 6.5, 5.5, INTR, INTA, HOLD, HLDA, READY, RESET IN, RESET OUT), and serial I/O (SID, SOD).","w":["ALE goes high in the first clock period of a machine cycle, to say that AD0 to AD7 carry the lower address byte.","Interrupt priority from highest: TRAP, RST 7.5, RST 6.5, RST 5.5, INTR."],"x":["Pins 21 to 28 are the high order address lines A8 to A15. Pins 12 to 19 are the multiplexed AD0 to AD7: they carry the low address byte first and then the data. Pin 40 is Vcc (+5 V) and pin 20 is Vss (ground). X1 and X2 take the crystal, and CLK OUT gives the operating clock.","Control and status signals: ALE (address latch enable) tells that AD0 to AD7 hold an address, so that an outside latch can keep it; RD and WR are the read and write signals; IO/M says whether the operation is for an I/O device or memory; S0 and S1 give the type of the machine cycle.","Interrupts: TRAP (highest priority, not maskable), RST 7.5, RST 6.5, RST 5.5 and INTR (lowest); INTA acknowledges INTR. HOLD and HLDA are used for direct memory access, READY makes the microprocessor wait for a slow device, RESET IN restarts it and RESET OUT shows that it is being reset. SID and SOD are the serial input and output data pins."],"e":"When a program reads a memory location, the 8085 puts the address on A8 to A15 and AD0 to AD7, raises ALE to let the latch catch the low byte, sets IO/M low for memory, and pulls RD low to read the data."}};
